1
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:12,320
Hi, I'm Eduardo Sebastián and together with Eduardo Montijano and Carlos Awes, we have made the work titled All in One: Certifiable Optimal Distributed Kalman Filter Under unknown correlations.

2
00:00:14,286 --> 00:00:28,452
So the problem of stage estimation is of paramount importance in several applications such that in this Narara Eco Village Smart Grid, in coordination in terms of drones, in active target tracking, or in the coming of the Internet of Things.

3
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:32,036
In all these cases, Kalman filter plays a central role.

4
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:39,812
And among other reasons, this is due to the fact that the Kalman filter is the optimal estimator in the mean square error sense.

5
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:58,000
This property is very well demonstrated in the centralized standard Kalman filter case, which is not possible to be applied in this kind of applications because it's not possible to have a central node collecting and processing the information from all the nodes of these big networks.

6
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:05,348
So the alternative is to try to find a distributed solution which preserves the stability and optimality properties of the original filter.

7
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:20,228
However, Orfati, some years ago, demonstrated that for the distributed common filter to be optimal, we need each node of the network to keep track of the whole network, which is not scalable nor implementable.

8
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:33,572
So the alternative is to try to find from the beginning a solution which accounts for that impossibility of keeping track of the whole network, which is technically called optimal estimation under uncorrelations.

9
00:01:33,806 --> 00:01:36,708
This yielded to what is called diffusion common filters.

10
00:01:38,318 --> 00:01:40,960
In this kind of filters, two are the main approaches.

11
00:01:41,262 --> 00:01:45,156
The first one is to be aware from the beginning that we are going to be suboptimal.

12
00:01:45,550 --> 00:01:48,836
The other is to try to resolve an NP hard optimization problem.

13
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:56,420
None of them tackle our objective of finding an optimality in the distributed common filter setting.

14
00:01:56,654 --> 00:01:58,308
So something else is needed.

15
00:01:59,374 --> 00:02:03,396
To do so, we propose certification and it works as follows.

16
00:02:04,014 --> 00:02:09,700
Our idea is to try to find a compromise between the benefits of both worlds.

17
00:02:10,894 --> 00:02:18,404
On one hand, we find a solution of a convex, relaxed solution of the original one, which is easy to implement and find.

18
00:02:18,734 --> 00:02:33,348
And then we develop a procedure to check if the solution of that suboptimal optimization, which is generally not optimal, is indeed equivalent to the original MPHL optimization problem solution we are trying to find.

19
00:02:33,870 --> 00:02:39,396
If this process is positive, then we are going to be able to say that we are being optimal indeed.

20
00:02:39,790 --> 00:02:41,252
And that's certification.

21
00:02:41,486 --> 00:02:42,596
And that's what we do.

22
00:02:43,662 --> 00:03:01,124
Our proposal with this work is code EKGF, the first distributed common filter with certifiability warranties, which means that under these warranties, we find an optimal filter estimator in the mean square error sense under a known correlations.

23
00:03:01,646 --> 00:03:06,020
Moreover, this optimality can be checked locally and online by each node.

24
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:26,692
And it is a globally asymptotically stable estimator, while preserving some other very interesting properties from other distributed common filters from the state of the art, like fully distribution, small communication burden, working with heterogeneous sensors, and an amazing property that no tuning is required.

25
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:36,644
So the problem in particular we are trying to solve is to find, estimate the state of a linear discrete system affected by zero mean Gaussian noise.

26
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:44,004
To do so, we have a network of nodes where each node is equipped with a linear sensor affected by zero mean Gaussian noise as well.

27
00:03:45,184 --> 00:03:48,704
Each node keeps track of two important quantities.

28
00:03:48,974 --> 00:03:52,036
The first one are the predicted and updated estimation.

29
00:03:52,206 --> 00:04:02,144
And associated with it, we have a matrix, an error covariance matrix, which is not possible to know in advance because we don't know the real state of the system.

30
00:04:02,224 --> 00:04:06,628
So we have their corresponding predicted and updated version of that matrix.

31
00:04:07,630 --> 00:04:12,864
So our proposal, code the KJF algorithm, is composed by five different steps.

32
00:04:13,024 --> 00:04:15,300
Four of them are very well studied.

33
00:04:15,424 --> 00:04:17,636
So I'm not going to go into details about them.

34
00:04:17,824 --> 00:04:23,268
The important one, the one we are interested in, is the middle step, the fusion of estimates.

35
00:04:23,790 --> 00:04:30,212
It is the tricky part because the fusion of estimates is affected by the correlation among estimations.

36
00:04:31,424 --> 00:04:39,332
And this is very important because this is the step which allows us to claim optimality and certifiability in our solution.

37
00:04:40,046 --> 00:04:46,148
Since it is the core of our proposal, is the thing I'm going to explain now in the talk.

38
00:04:47,184 --> 00:04:58,756
So imagine we have a node where at some instant we have to fusion two estimates, S1 and S2, which are the inverse of their covariance matrices, and represented by these blue ellipsoids.

39
00:04:59,150 --> 00:05:02,116
The region in red is the intersection of these ellipsoids.

40
00:05:02,224 --> 00:05:14,628
And the important thing about this polytope is that irrespective of the correlation among estimates, the result, this green ellipsoid, for example, is going to be inside this polytope.

41
00:05:15,310 --> 00:05:22,084
But since we don't know that correlation, the best we can do is to try to find the smallest ellipsoid which surrounds that polytope.

42
00:05:22,702 --> 00:05:32,420
We are being consistent because independently on the correlation among estimates, the green ellipsoid is going to be inside our purple desired ellipsoid.

43
00:05:33,358 --> 00:05:38,144
And we are being optimal because we are finding the smallest one surrounding completely the polytope.

44
00:05:38,414 --> 00:05:44,516
This is the outer Launer John ellipsoid, and that's what we are doing in our fusion of estimates in code the KF algorithm.

45
00:05:45,344 --> 00:05:55,940
The mathematical translation of this objective is this optimization problem, which as you can see is not up, is non-convex due to this NP hard constraint, the rank one constraint.

46
00:05:56,224 --> 00:05:58,980
If we drop it out, we find a convex relaxation.

47
00:05:59,424 --> 00:06:09,956
However, none of these optimization problems gives us a the result gives us a direct result that can be used in our code-KF algorithm.

48
00:06:10,144 --> 00:06:15,780
We need an optimal estimation and an optimal error covariance as a result to do our estimation.

49
00:06:16,624 --> 00:06:29,540
So the alternative we have used is a different converse relaxation, which has this form, which is the only one which gives us an optimal estimation and associated error covariance matrix as a result.

50
00:06:30,464 --> 00:06:34,784
So now, up to now, we have three different optimization problems.

51
00:06:34,944 --> 00:06:38,304
The first one used directly in the Code KF algorithm.

52
00:06:38,464 --> 00:06:41,828
The two others are going to be used to certify optimality.

53
00:06:42,158 --> 00:06:53,508
So our purpose now is to describe how we do this and what is the relationships among these three optimization problems to find certifiability and optimality.

54
00:06:54,478 --> 00:06:58,384
This is done and demonstrated in proposition one about certifiability.

55
00:06:58,464 --> 00:07:02,724
And two are the main relationships to do this certification process.

56
00:07:03,310 --> 00:07:11,264
The first one is that we have found that the solution of the co-DKF optimization problem and that of the relaxed problem are equivalent.

57
00:07:11,344 --> 00:07:13,252
So in some sense, we can exchange them.

58
00:07:14,734 --> 00:07:19,876
The other interesting thing is that we have found that the solution of this relaxed convex problem.

59
00:07:20,526 --> 00:07:25,296
Is equivalent to the solution of the original non-convex problem if two conditions hold.

60
00:07:25,536 --> 00:07:34,628
The first one is that the solution of this optimization problem has rank one, as in the constraint of the original non-convex problem, DCI equal to one.

61
00:07:35,456 --> 00:07:46,596
But among all the possible solutions of this optimization problem with rank one, the one we are interested in, the one which is equivalent, is the one that accomplishes the following.

62
00:07:47,150 --> 00:07:54,976
The trace we are maximizing here must be equal to the inverse of the small staging value of this error covariance matrix.

63
00:07:55,278 --> 00:08:04,516
And this condition is needed because the original non-convex problem is bounded by this inverse of the minimum A value of the error covariance matrix of this problem.

64
00:08:05,038 --> 00:08:10,656
So when the two conditions hold, then we have reconstructed the original non-convex problem.

65
00:08:10,736 --> 00:08:18,016
So we can use this problem, the first one of Code DKIF, to find the optimal estimates and associated error covariance.

66
00:08:18,096 --> 00:08:26,692
And then solve the relaxed convex problem, checking the conditions to see if we are truly in the original non-convex problem solution.

67
00:08:27,696 --> 00:08:38,576
So after resolving these nice properties about certifiability, we have the tools to demonstrate two other very important properties.

68
00:08:38,736 --> 00:08:45,536
The first one is that Codic IF is a globally and synthetically stable algorithm irrespective of the result of the optimization.

69
00:08:45,616 --> 00:09:03,684
And besides, if the certification is positive, then we can say that our estimator is optimal in the mean square error sense without surrendering other important properties such as this fully distribution, we need small messages, and notice that we don't need any tanning at all.

70
00:09:04,238 --> 00:09:19,716
To verify all these properties, we have conducted several experiments over this system with its parameters, comparing the performance of our code EKF against three state-of-the-art distributed Kalman filters and the centralized Kalman filter as a baseline.

71
00:09:20,718 --> 00:09:23,588
Two particular experiments have been conducted.

72
00:09:23,856 --> 00:09:28,576
The first one picks randomly if a sensor is of low quality or high quality.

73
00:09:28,656 --> 00:09:35,844
And the second one picks a random sensor and decides that it is the only sensor of high quality of the whole network.

74
00:09:36,016 --> 00:09:52,100
The purpose of this, the motivation under this experiment is to verify that CodEKF has a very good performance even in highly noisy sparser scenarios, where the noise of the sensors is a deal.

75
00:09:53,696 --> 00:10:09,508
And as you can see, in both experiment one and experiment two, code EKF is the best distributed Kamman filter in performance with an order of magnitude of difference with respect to the other three state-of-the-art distributed Kaman filters, this red trace.

76
00:10:09,934 --> 00:10:16,736
Okay, and as you can see, it is also pretty close to the centralized Kalman filter in both transient and steady state.

77
00:10:17,134 --> 00:10:24,612
But we have also need to verify if even if we are fast and accurate, if we are indeed being optimal.

78
00:10:24,896 --> 00:10:29,892
So we have checked the conditions about certifiability in both experiments.

79
00:10:30,382 --> 00:10:38,496
In a 96% of the cases and a 93% of the cases, the rank one condition holds in both experiment one and experiment two.

80
00:10:38,736 --> 00:10:52,836
And as you can see, with this row almost always equal to one in both experiment one and experiment two, almost always we are near one, which means that we are almost always being optimal.

81
00:10:53,216 --> 00:10:58,336
So our relaxation, our certification, can be safe to be tight.

82
00:10:59,214 --> 00:11:06,788
So to conclude with, we can see that Code DKF is the first certifiable optimal distributed common filter under our correlations.

83
00:11:07,216 --> 00:11:13,796
It is robust over heterogeneous sensor networks, needs minimal communication burden, and we don't need any tanning.

84
00:11:14,096 --> 00:11:23,492
Moreover, it's a global asymptotically stable estimator, and it is optimal under positive certification, which is almost always accomplished.

85
00:11:24,416 --> 00:11:33,056
And finally, we have exhibit superior performance with respect to other state of the art distributed common filters, especially in sparse, highly noisy scenarios.

86
00:11:34,094 --> 00:11:37,956
Finally, ending up, I would like to acknowledge the support of our founders.

87
00:11:39,118 --> 00:11:42,180
Thank you very much, and I'm open for any questions you may have.

